---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import LearningCard from "../../components/LearningCard.astro";
import ArticleCard from "../../components/ArticleCard.astro";
import { SITE, fullArticles } from "../../data/site";
import { EDITORIAL_AUTHOR_SCHEMA, PUBLISHER_SCHEMA } from "../../data/editorialGovernance";

const title = "Four Noble Truths Explained for Beginners";
const description =
  "Learn the Four Noble Truths in simple language: suffering, the causes of suffering, the possibility of release, and the path of practice.";
const canonicalPath = "/learn/four-noble-truths/";
const truthCards = [
  { label: "1. Dukkha", href: "/learn/buddhist-dictionary/dukkha/", description: "Life includes stress, pain, dissatisfaction, and instability." },
  { label: "2. Cause", href: "/learn/buddhism-101/the-four-noble-truths-explained/", description: "Craving, clinging, and confusion add suffering to experience." },
  { label: "3. Release", href: "/learn/buddhist-dictionary/nirvana/", description: "The fires of greed, hatred, and delusion can cool." },
  { label: "4. Path", href: "/learn/eightfold-path/", description: "The Noble Eightfold Path trains wisdom, conduct, and the mind." }
];
const truthTasks = [
  {
    truth: "Dukkha",
    task: "Understand it",
    explanation: "Recognize pain, stress, dissatisfaction, and the instability of what we depend on. The task is careful understanding, not pessimism or self-blame."
  },
  {
    truth: "Origin",
    task: "Abandon its cause",
    explanation: "Notice craving that demands pleasure, continued existence, or escape. Craving is not a claim that every illness, loss, or injustice was caused by the sufferer."
  },
  {
    truth: "Cessation",
    task: "Realize it",
    explanation: "The teaching points to the ending of craving and the release that follows. A pleasant break is welcome, but it is not the whole meaning of cessation."
  },
  {
    truth: "Path",
    task: "Develop it",
    explanation: "Cultivate the Noble Eightfold Path. The path is an ongoing training in view, intention, conduct, effort, mindfulness, and collectedness—not one quick technique."
  }
];
const relatedArticles = ["four-noble-truths-explained-simply", "four-noble-truths-explained", "what-is-buddhism-beginner-guide"]
  .map((slug) => fullArticles.find((article) => article.slug === slug))
  .filter(Boolean);
const sourceLinks = [
  {
    label: "Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta (SN 56.11)",
    href: "https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato",
    note: "An early-discourse source for the truths and for the distinct task associated with each truth."
  },
  {
    label: "Magga-vibhanga Sutta (SN 45.8)",
    href: "https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato",
    note: "Analyzes the Noble Eightfold Path that forms the fourth truth. Translation terminology varies by translator."
  }
];
const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: title,
    description,
    url: `${SITE.url}${canonicalPath}`,
    author: EDITORIAL_AUTHOR_SCHEMA,
    publisher: PUBLISHER_SCHEMA
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: SITE.url },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Learn", item: `${SITE.url}/learn/` },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Four Noble Truths", item: `${SITE.url}${canonicalPath}` }
    ]
  }
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath={canonicalPath} type="article" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article>
    <header class="page-hero">
      <div class="container prose">
        <a class="back-link" href="/learn/">Learn</a>
        <span class="eyebrow">Core teaching hub</span>
        <h1>The Four Noble Truths Explained for Beginners</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          The Four Noble Truths are a compassionate framework for understanding suffering, its causes, the possibility
          of release, and the path of practice.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section section--tight">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="grid grid--2">
          {truthCards.map((item) => <LearningCard item={item} />)}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-band">
      <div class="container prose">
        <h2>What the Four Truths Ask a Practitioner to Do</h2>
        <p>
          The truths are not merely four statements to agree with. In SN 56.11, each has a different task: dukkha is to
          be understood, its origin is to be abandoned, cessation is to be realized, and the path is to be developed.
          That distinction prevents a common mistake—trying to “get rid of” painful experience before understanding it.
        </p>
        <div class="truth-tasks">
          {truthTasks.map((item) => (
            <div>
              <span>{item.truth}</span>
              <h3>{item.task}</h3>
              <p>{item.explanation}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <h2>Dukkha Is Broader Than Obvious Pain</h2>
        <p>
          Dukkha includes unmistakable pain, but it also includes the strain of depending on changing conditions for
          lasting security. A pleasant experience can be enjoyable and still be unreliable: it ends, changes, or cannot
          be held on command. The teaching does not ask a grieving or injured person to dismiss pain as “just
          attachment.” It asks for an honest view of experience and of the craving that can add another layer of demand.
        </p>
        <h2>A Worked Example: Criticism at Work</h2>
        <p>
          Imagine receiving critical feedback on work you cared about. The first truth begins with what is present:
          embarrassment, uncertainty, and a tightening body. The second looks for the demand beneath the reaction—“I
          must be seen as competent” or “this feeling must disappear now.” The third does not promise that the feedback
          vanishes; it points to the possibility of not feeding that demand. The fourth asks what to develop next:
          accurate view, an intention not to retaliate, truthful speech, and enough mindfulness to hear what is useful.
        </p>
        <p>
          This sequence is Echo Buddha's editorial application of the traditional framework, not wording from the
          discourse. Its purpose is to show why the truths work together rather than turning them into four slogans.
        </p>
        <h2>A Focused Practice</h2>
        <p>
          With one manageable difficulty, write four short lines: what hurts; what the mind is demanding; what it would
          mean not to feed that demand for one moment; and which path factor would support the next response. Do not use
          the exercise to blame yourself for pain or to avoid action on injustice, illness, or unsafe conditions.
        </p>
        <h2>Source Notes</h2>
        <p>
          The framework and four tasks are grounded in SN 56.11. The definitions, workplace example, and practice are
          concise paraphrase and original editorial explanation. No translation is reproduced here. For formal study,
          compare translations and learn how different Buddhist traditions teach dukkha, cessation, and the path.
        </p>
        <ul class="source-list">
          {sourceLinks.map((source) => (
            <li>
              <a href={source.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{source.label}</a>
              <span>{source.note}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="eyebrow">Related reading</span>
        <h2>Continue Learning</h2>
        <div class="grid grid--3">
          {relatedArticles.map((article) => <ArticleCard article={article} />)}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .back-link {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .section-band {
    background: rgba(247, 240, 229, 0.64);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  }

  .source-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.65rem;
    list-style: none;
    padding-left: 0;
  }

  .source-list li {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-gold);
    padding-left: 0.85rem;
  }

  .source-list span {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    margin-top: 0.15rem;
  }

  .truth-tasks {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    margin-block: 1.5rem;
  }

  .truth-tasks > div {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-gold);
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }

  .truth-tasks span {
    color: var(--color-green);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .truth-tasks h3 {
    margin: 0.2rem 0 0.35rem;
  }

  .truth-tasks p {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
